 ## Overview
-This family defines how to compute the color and appearance of the isosurface detected by the parent method [G]. It solves the problem of visualizing the geometric structure and material properties of the extracted surface by applying lighting models, curvature-based effects, and color enhancements to the intersection points [G]. It distinguishes itself from direct volume rendering by ignoring contributions from voxels behind the surface (opaque shading) and from immediate shading methods by potentially decoupling the computation of high-order differential properties (like curvature) into separate rendering passes for higher accuracy and advanced effects [867-deferred-shading-for-isosurfaces.md].
